    "explanation": "Carbon monoxide is a colorless, odorless gas that binds to hemoglobin with an affinity more than 200 times stronger than oxygen. Inhalation of CO is a major cause of morbidity and mortality in the United States. A dual-wave (660 nm and 940 nm) pulse oximeter is incapable of distinguishing CO hemoglobin from oxyhemoglobin, but the distinction is easily made in the clinical laboratory with a co- oximeter. Significant quantities of methemoglobin would result in a saturation of 85% of the pulse oximeter. The slight right shift from a mild acidemia would be insufficient to account for 90% saturation in the face of a PaO2 of 190. Furthermore, the pulse oximeter reading would be nearly the same as the co-oximeter value (Miller: Miller’s Anesthesia, ed 8, pp 2679–2680; Miller: Basics of Anesthesia, ed 7, p 341; Hines: Stoelting’s Anesthesia and Co-Existing Disease, ed 7, pp 631–632)."
